r/value "exact value" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346467/field-3/b75f0df421c1d3c2be4949f3e1d65d645758732d10c6da03e9fdeb34a06af273" :answer/id #uuid "372694fc-ab4f-5700-a70e-724aeabc781e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "underestimate" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-346467/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1f5d297-2d85-55e8-8686-c1c67cd8f3f9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-346467/field-3/b75f0df421c1d3c2be4949f3e1d65d645758732d10c6da03e9fdeb34a06af273" :answer-field/choices ["q-346467/field-3/0a4fb32c5ead7a42793f1609f7129a2d005c40f06f2ac02448bf0930df5b4d72" "q-346467/field-3/d0f1bd886c1f943d290287f9af1f6126289cb0f265f1f6ce7fca82ca9c79a47f" "q-346467/field-3/b75f0df421c1d3c2be4949f3e1d65d645758732d10c6da03e9fdeb34a06af273"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-346467" :question/id #uuid "277725a3-237e-5051-ae07-4598fad2f8f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-346467" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$The estimated area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/assets/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-346467/field-1" "q-346467/field-2" "q-346467/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-346467"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
